---
import Shell from '../components/Martlet.astro';import {localPath as href} from '../paths';
const page={id:'site-home',title:'Martlet for Astro',eyebrow:'The Astro reference',summary:'Astro components and examples for publishing, services, and applications.'};
---
<Shell page={page} href={href} base={import.meta.env.BASE_URL} renderContent={false}>
<h2>A smaller engine. The same language.</h2><p>This is a Astro reference application built from the canonical <strong>Martlet reference shell</strong>. Its approved imagery, typography and stylesheet retain the same visible language as the Jekyll reference. Astro supplies native components and routes; the browser receives ordinary HTML.</p>
<h3>Three compositions to inspect</h3><ul><li><a href={href('/language/')}>The language</a> uses a wide document with a left-hand index.</li><li><a href={href('/examples/article/')}>A field essay</a> uses a narrow reading column, inline contents, date, author, and tags.</li><li><a href={href('/setup/')}>Use this template</a> explains how to build it and replace the content.</li></ul>
<h2>A shell with a job</h2><p>The masthead holds the title and context. Navigation leads to destinations. An index links within the current document. The footer closes the page. Those relationships survive a different rendering engine.</p><details><summary>A note from the workshop</summary><p>No feet. Excellent footing.</p></details>
<h2>The complete example library</h2><p><a href={href('/examples/')}>Explore all 50 page recipes</a>: publishing, projects, collections, people, documentation, commerce, events, reports, support, recovery, and application interfaces. The same fictional institute and content appear in both themes.</p><p><a href={href('/examples/integrations/')}>Read about integration boundaries</a> before connecting forms, accounts, devices, or transactions to a live service.</p>
</Shell>
